[Music]  finally back  in the middle of the action  [Applause]  football fans from across europe are  coming together beer is flowing  and all of it is happening without face  masks as if nothing ever happened  we are worried about the delta variant  the delta variant means we have to stay  cautious  the delta variant is coming it spreads  faster than previous variants  so is the party over should europe be  back  on a crisis footing is the vaccine  rollout not fast enough  what's the verdict for europe is the  glass half empty  or half full hello and welcome to dw's  covert 19 special i'm daniel winter  in berlin delta delta plus even a gamma  a veritable alphabet soup of coronavirus  variants  let's take a closer look at delta which  originated in india  it spreads faster than the original in  europe it's becoming the predominant  strain  delta accounts for 97 of all sequenced  positive test samples in the uk  and is blamed for sending infections  rising once again  it's also dominant in portugal and  russia elsewhere the variant is  spreading quickly in austria where it  represents  one of every two cases and it's getting  a foothold in the czech republic too  in germany recent data shows delta is  around 37 percent  of new infections but the country's  infectious disease center recently  estimated  it was already higher perhaps 50 percent  some predict the delta variant could  account for 90 percent of all new cases  by the end of august  that may sound bad but remember there's  no evidence that the disease is  any more dangerous the scientific  evidence shows the spread of delta is  cause for caution  but not for panic  getting the first shot of a vaccine  against 19 is likely to come as a big  relief to those lucky enough to have one  but what happens when new variants come  along  the world of science has some good news  on this front  against the delta variant biontech was  found to be 88  effective at preventing symptomatic  disease  that's according to a study by public  health england  astrazeneca was 60 effective  as for the delta plus variant not enough  data is available to know how it  interacts with immunizations  scientists in india and worldwide are  looking into it  but researchers say it's unlikely it'll  outfox the current jabs  as well as the vaccine's effectiveness  against the variants  another big question is how long the  shots protect us for  according to a nature paper it could be  a good while  that's for mrna jabs like biontech  moderna  the figure of the immune response found  in some biotech  vaccinated people was said by  researchers to be a strong indication  that protection could last for years  that's against covid19 as it looks at  the moment  if the virus changes out of all  recognition it might be a different ball  game  but that may or may not happen in the  future  for now the vaccines available are our  best way out of this pandemic  [Applause]  and joining us now is dr andreas  bergtala a  virologist at the austrian academy of  sciences  so alpha beta gamma  epsilon delta and delta plus even now  how worried do we need to be about all  these variants  i don't know whether word is the right  word i would say we have to be alert  because we know that the virus keeps  changing this is something we have been  observing since the beginning of the  pandemic and we do know that there are  certain variants which have become more  infectious  um sometimes also acquiring immune  escape properties so evading the immune  response a bit better  and these are all properties that one  has to factor  in when we try to counter the virus by  for example  particular measurements such as social  distancing  or vaccination and why is it that we're  now suddenly hearing about so many  variants showing up are they  really just coming to light just now are  we just starting to understand them more  what's the case there i think it's a  very interesting question that  um science itself and the scientists i  think don't fully  understand yet what is clear is that  many of these variants they actually  take quite some time until the surface  so just to give you one example  delta has been first sequenced in  september 2020.  so it took more than half a year for  this virus to eventually  hit the uk and eventually now also rest  of continental europe and the world  and so this is one thing it takes time  for these variants  at the same time also we have evidence  that we see over the last  months that evolution might be  accelerating so that somehow  sarskoff ii might be faster  in acquiring mutations and these are all  processes that scientists try to  understand better  okay and uh looking at what the possible  near future could be  dealing with all of these variants the  uk says that they're aiming for total  unlocking  on june the 19th but delta case numbers  are still very high so  is this the right thing to do in your  opinion now do we just have to accept  that there is going to be no  good time to unlock and just grit our  teeth and get through it  i mean to cite some well-known  scientists in the uk uh  this is going to be a big experiment and  it's always  i think difficult or impossible to to  know up front  what's going to happen but i think  chances are that this will  lead to a big spike in increase of  infections  and the question is how much will this  affect hospitalization rate  and and severe cases of of of clinical  disease  and we do know that in general uh the  current  uh vaccines including astrosenica that  is used wide in the uk but also biotech  for example has  a very high protection against severe  disease  and this is something i think that one  has to see since there's still  a quite a big share of the population  including the younger ones  that have been not vaccinated whether  this experiment turns out to be the  right way  right well i'd just like to pick up on  that actually you know israeli  researchers just recently have said  that the biontech jab is less effective  against the delta variant than  previously thought which is what  i guess you were referring to there what  does this mean for the fight against  covert 19  i think one has to distinguish between  uh the different effects of  of being vaccinated on the one hand a  vaccine is there to protect yourself  so it's kind of an egoistic action  and this is still giving you a very high  protection  against the delta variant the other  point is  you would like to have a vaccine to  prevent the spread of the virus so  from being infected in the first place  and then transmitting the virus to other  persons  and there seems to be uh some data  coming from israel  that maybe those people have been  vaccinated with biotech quite some time  ago already  and this is also important to factor in  time  that those people might still get  infected although  they don't succumb to the disease or  develop severe disease so  i think overall there's still good news  that the vaccines are working against  delta  but it could be that they are not as  effective anymore in reducing the spread  so um are we kind of wasting our time  with too much alarmism around the  variants  um i'm not sure i think we should not be  surprised anymore after one and a half  years of the pandemic um that there will  be new variants coming  uh around and whether it's delta epsilon  lambda  um the greek alphabet has 24 letters so  let's see when we reach omega  but i think these are things that we can  expect the big question with all of  these  new arriving variants is do they have  changed properties are they more  infectious do they lead to more  disease are they potentially escaping  uh the immune response speed is from  natural infection or also from  vaccination okay  and this is something i guess that has  to be um  assessed for each and every very  absolutely important questions  for the future for us to ask there dr  andreas bergtala of the austrian academy  of sciences thank you very much for  joining us  thank you for having me and it's not  just  us who ask the questions now it's your  turn it's the part of the program where  we put  one of your questions to our science  correspondent derek williams  does the vaccine block the virus or  simply keep it from causing severe  disease  okay i've talked about this several  times in the past but maybe it's time  for an update on  on what we know now as you rightly point  out mark  initial trials carried out with vaccines  that have been approved for use so far  they were set up to look at the vaccine  safety and how well the vaccines were  able to block the development  of symptomatic disease in recipients  in particular severe disease and and  they're really  really good at that though they're not  perfect what are called  breakthrough cases do occur those are  people  who are fully vaccinated that somehow  subsequently  test positive for the virus but when you  look at the numbers  breakthrough infections are few and far  between  and most of the people it happens to  get asymptomatic or mild cases  of covet breakthrough cases that end up  in the hospital  are even fewer and and far betweener  if you can say that so a big win  on the primary goal of stopping bad  disease  but it's taken time to gather data on a  major  secondary goal which is stopping virus  transmission  do vaccines also provide at least some  degree of what's called  sterilizing immunity that's when  if you're exposed to a pathogen after  getting your shots  your immune system is so primed to wipe  it out that the pathogen basically has  no chance  to gain even a temporary foothold  and since it can't replicate in you you  can't give it to someone else  evidence is now piling up that if you're  vaccinated  then your risk of catching covet 19  in a measurable way is a lot lower  so the logical assumption is that the  chances you might subsequently pass it  along  are also a lot lower than they would be  for someone who is  unvaccinated but a small chance  isn't a zero chance the fact page at  johns hopkins university expresses  our current level of knowledge very well  i think  it says vaccines are  likely to reduce the risk of virus  transmission but  probably not completely for everyone  [Music]  and that's all for this edition of our  covert 19 special for more information  about the pandemic you can check out the  covert 19 section  on our website dw.com i'm daniel winter  in berlin until next time thanks for  watching and take care  [Music]

